 SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: C=C <-> [C:21]=[CH:22] <-> [20*]C([27*])=C[23*], \\n2. secondary hydroxyl:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: O <-> [OH:31] <-> [30*]O, \\n3. ketone:\\n   Count:2\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: C=O <-> [C:7]=[O:8] <-> [6*]C([9*])=O,  C=O <-> [C:23]=[O:24] <-> [22*]C([25*])=O, \\n4. carboxylic ester:\\n   Count:2\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: C(=O)O <-> [C:3](=[O:4])[O:5] <-> [2*]C(=O)O[6*],  OC=O <-> [O:10][C:11]=[O:12] <-> [9*]OC([13*])=O, \\n", "function_name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCCC(=O)O[C@]1(C(=O)COC(=O)CC)CC[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)CC[C@]4(C)[C@H]3[C@@H](O)C[C@@]21C"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nSo we have alkene, secondary hydroxyl, ketones, and esters. The molecule is likely a steroid derivative with multiple polar groups.\\n\\nNow, let\'s get molar refractivity (MR) which relates to lipophilicity and size.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_mr", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCCC(=O)O[C@]1(C(=O)COC(=O)CC)CC[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)CC[C@]4(C)[C@H]3[C@@H](O)C[C@@]21C"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Wildman\\u2013Crippen molar refractivity (MR) estimate: 128.0878", "function_name": "get_mol_mr", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCCC(=O)O[C@]1(C(=O)COC(=O)CC)CC[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)CC[C@]4(C)[C@H]3[C@@H](O)C[C@@]21C"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMR 128, high.\\n\\nNow, fraction of sp3 carbons (CSP3) as a measure of 3D character.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_fraction_csp3", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCCC(=O)O[C@]1(C(=O)COC(=O)CC)CC[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)CC[C@]4(C)[C@H]3[C@@H](O)C[C@@]21C"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Fraction of sp3 carbons: 0.7857", "function_name": "get_fraction_csp3", "arguments":