ased predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "predict_pka",\\n      "description": "Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "estimate_logd",\\n      "description": "Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "ph": {\\n            "type": "number",\\n            "description": "Target pH (default: 7.4)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "score_structural_alerts",\\n      "description": "Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "alert_library": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "enum": [\\n              "all",\\n              "pains",\\n              "pains_a",\\n              "pains_b",\\n              "pains_c",\\n              "brenk",\\n              "nih",\\n              "zinc",\\n              "chembl",\\n              "chembl_bms",\\n              "chembl_lint",\\n              "chembl_mlsmr"\\n            ],\\n            "description": "Alert library to screen against (default: all)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_labute_asa",\\n      "description": "Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return fraction of aromatic atoms (aromatic atoms / total atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return number of aromatic atoms.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aliphatic_rings",\\n      "description": "Return aliphatic ring count using RDKit Lipinski.NumAliphaticRings.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_saturated_rings",\\n      "description": "Return saturated ring count using RDKit Lipinski.NumSaturatedRings.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_positive_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of cationic centers (atoms with formal charge > 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_negative_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of anionic centers (atoms with formal charge < 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n]\\nuser\\nInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: As a membrane separating circulating blood and brain extracellular fluid, the blood-brain barrier (BBB) is the protection layer that blocks most foreign drugs. Thus the ability of a drug to penetrate the barrier to deliver to the site of action forms a crucial challenge in development of drugs for central nervous system.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) does not cross the BBB (B) crosses the BBB\\nDrug SMILES: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1.[Br-]\\n\\nPlease think step by step and use tools when necessary (**Don\'t use the same tool more than once**). Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"\\nassistant\\n<think>\\nThe user asks to predict BBB penetration for a drug SMILES: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1.[Br-]. This looks like a salt: a cationic species with a bromide counterion. The SMILES includes a dot, indicating separate components: the cation and the anion. The cation is likely a quaternary ammonium or pyridinium derivative: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1. That appears to be a methylcarbamate ester attached to a pyridine ring with a methylated nitrogen (quaternary). Actually, the SMILES: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1. Let\'s parse: CN(C)C(=O)O is a methylcarbamate group (dimethylcarbamate). Then c1ccc[n+](C)c1 is a pyridine ring with a positively charged nitrogen (quaternary) and a methyl substituent on that nitrogen? Actually, [n+](C) indicates a positively charged nitrogen with a methyl attached. So the structure is a pyridinium cation with a methylcarbamate ester attached via oxygen. The bromide is counterion.\\n\\nWe need to predict BBB penetration. Generally, compounds that are charged (positively or negatively) have difficulty crossing the BBB due to the lipid bilayer. However, some charged compounds can cross via transporters. Quaternary ammonium compounds are generally not able to cross the BBB because they are permanently charged and cannot partition into lipid membranes. However, there are exceptions: some quaternary ammonium compounds can cross via active transport or if the charge is internalized via endocytosis. But generally, BBB penetration is poor for permanently charged species.\\n\\nWe can compute some properties to inform the prediction: molecular weight, logP, TPSA, HBD/HBA, etc. Let\'s compute some descriptors.\\n\\nFirst, let\'s get molecular weight, logP, TPSA, HBD, HBA, rotatable bonds, formal charge, etc. We\'ll use the tools.\\n\\nBut note: the SMILES includes a dot separating cation and anion. The tools likely expect a single molecule. We should desalt the SMILES to get the parent organic fragment. Use remove_salts to get the cation. Let\'s do that.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "remove_salts", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1.[Br-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Canonical SMILES of the largest organic fragment after salt removal: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1", "function_name": "remove_salts", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1.[Br-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNow we have the cation SMILES: CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1. This is the parent molecule. Let\'s compute properties.\\n\\nFirst, molecular weight:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 181.2150", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nExact molecular weight:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_exact_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Exact molecular weight (Daltons): 181.0972", "function_name": "get_exact_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHeavy atom count:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of heavy atoms: 13", "function_name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nLogP:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_logp", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "cLogP estimate: 0.5715", "function_name": "get_mol_logp", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)C(=O)Oc1ccc[n+](C)c1"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nTPSA:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_tpsa", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)C